{"slug":"actions-route-handlers","category":"nextjs","title":"server actions vs route handlers","description":"choose an interface for the caller you actually have.","minutes":6,"level":"study plan","tags":["nextjs","week-3","interview-prep"],"questions":[{"question":"does using a server action remove the need for authorization?","answer":"no. treat the action as a callable mutation boundary. authenticate and authorize the specific operation on the server."},{"question":"when does a route handler fit better?","answer":"when a webhook, external client, or other caller needs an explicit http api rather than react’s action integration."}],"facts":["validate request bodies at runtime.","return safe, consistent errors.","shared mutation rules belong below the transport interface."],"source":"https://nextjs.org/docs/app/getting-started/mutating-data","code":"// app/api/entries/route.ts — illustrative server helpers\nexport async function post(request: request) {\n  const user = await requireuser();\n  const input = await request.json().catch(() => null);\n  const parsed = entryschema.safeparse(input);\n  if (!parsed.success) {\n    return response.json({ error: \"invalid entry\" }, { status: 400 });\n  }\n  const entry = await createentryforuser(user.id, parsed.data);\n  return response.json({ id: entry.id }, { status: 201 });\n}","codelanguage":"ts","origin":"study-plan","planweek":3,"planpages":[3],"quizdistractors":["yes. a hidden action can never be called without the matching button.","yes. react automatically enforces your business permissions."]} ## the idea

a server action is a server function integrated with react-driven mutations. a route handler implements an http endpoint with an explicit method, request, and response. both cross a trust boundary and require input validation and access checks.

## why it matters

use an action for an app form when that integration fits. use a route handler for webhooks, external clients, or an http contract you intend to expose. do not duplicate the business rules in both; keep validation and mutation logic in a shared server layer.

## a practical example

```ts
// app/api/entries/route.ts — illustrative server helpers
export async function post(request: request) {
  const user = await requireuser();
  const input = await request.json().catch(() => null);
  const parsed = entryschema.safeparse(input);
  if (!parsed.success) {
    return response.json({ error: "invalid entry" }, { status: 400 });
  }
  const entry = await createentryforuser(user.id, parsed.data);
  return response.json({ id: entry.id }, { status: 201 });
}
```

## common mistakes

do not assume a hidden button protects a server function. the permission check must run at the actual data or mutation boundary.

## try it yourself

model one create-entry operation as a form action and as an http handler. keep one shared authorization and mutation function.

